Yes—with one important biological correction that actually makes the pattern more interesting.

Hermit crabs hatch as tiny free-swimming larvae, usually zoeae, not as miniature shell-less hermit crabs crawling around looking for houses. They pass through several larval stages in the plankton, then metamorphose into a bottom-dwelling juvenile. Around that transition, the vulnerable young crab begins seeking a suitable empty gastropod shell. From then on, as it grows, it repeatedly has to upgrade.

And that is the curious part you’re noticing: evolution has produced an organism whose normal adult phenotype is functionally incomplete without a structure manufactured by an entirely different evolutionary lineage.

The crab does not inherit the shell genetically. It inherits shell-seeking machinery—behavior, sensory preferences, abdominal morphology, gripping appendages, withdrawal reflexes, shell-assessment behavior, etc. Evolution effectively bets on an environmental regularity:

gastropods exist → gastropods die → shells persist → some shells become available → selection can exploit that persistent resource.

Not consciously or prospectively, of course. Hermit-crab ancestors that could exploit abundant empty shells gained protection, and selection progressively specialized them for that niche. But from the perspective of the resulting system, your formulation is strikingly apt: the animal is born into a world that is expected to contain part of its eventual body plan.

And it gets stranger. Shells aren’t merely present or absent. They’re a distributed housing market. Good shells are scarce. Crabs inspect them, fight over them, steal them, modify them, and sometimes form vacancy chains: several crabs line up by size around a newly available shell; the largest upgrades, freeing its old shell for the next crab, whose old shell becomes available to the next, and so forth.

So there are at least three organisms/processes entangled:

snail → manufactures durable structure → snail dies → structure becomes environmental capital → hermit crab appropriates it → crab eventually relinquishes it → another crab inherits the opportunity.

That gives you a broader evolutionary pattern worth keeping: an organism’s functional phenotype can extend beyond what its own genome constructs. Evolution can outsource part of the phenotype to sufficiently reliable structures in the environment.

Beavers exploit wood. Birds inherit no nest but inherit algorithms for constructing one. Humans are born expecting an enormous extracorporeal inheritance—language, caregivers, tools, institutions, accumulated knowledge. Hermit crabs occupy an especially beautiful intermediate case because another species literally manufactures a component that subsequently looks almost like part of the crab’s anatomy.

The shell is simultaneously environment, artifact, resource, armor, constraint, and inherited opportunity.

And there’s a deeper comparative-morphology question hiding there:

What things appear incomplete when examined as isolated organisms, but become complete when the unit of analysis expands to include their reliable environment?

Once you change the boundary of the organism/system, a lot of apparent deficiencies may turn into interfaces.